<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="sku-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="sku-title">How much stock detail is worth keeping?</h2>
      <p>Tracking every item can cost more than it saves, while tracking nothing leaves you guessing about margin and stock. Most wholesalers fall somewhere between. These options are our general analysis, so ask an accountant which fits.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="deliver-grid">
      <article><h3>Item by item</h3><p>Every product has a code and a quantity. This gives the clearest picture, and suits fewer lines or costlier goods. It needs billing software and steady entry.</p></article>
      <article><h3>By group</h3><p>Items are grouped by supplier, type and GST rate. This is lighter and still shows margin by group. It only works if a group never mixes tax rates.</p></article>
      <article><h3>By carton</h3><p>Stock is counted by the carton, and only opened cartons are tracked more closely. This suits goods that mostly move sealed. Broken cartons need a clear rule.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="rate-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="rate-title">One invoice, several tax rates</h2>
      <p>A mixed order can include toys, stationery, plastics and imitation jewellery. Each can carry its own rate, and the rate follows the item and its code, not the shop. Grouping them as "general goods" is where errors start.</p>
      <p class="para">Rule 46 of the CGST Rules lists what a tax invoice must show, including the HSN code, description, quantity, and tax rate and amount. It also requires an unregistered buyer's name and address where the taxable value is ₹50,000 or more. Check how notifications set the number of HSN digits you need.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Before the festive rush</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Agree the stock and party balances before the peak, while there is time</li>
        <li>Decide how extra staff and cash collections will be recorded</li>
        <li>Plan how bills from a busy day reach the books within days</li>
        <li>Ask when supplier payments will fall, compared with your collections</li>
        <li>Count leftover seasonal stock afterwards, and value it honestly</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="import-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="import-title">If you import directly</h2>
      <p>Buying through a Delhi importer is ordinary trade. Importing yourself adds a separate trail. Keep the bill of entry, duty paid, freight and clearing charges with the supplier's invoice, so each consignment's full landed cost can be worked out.</p>
      <p class="para">Ask how landed cost should reach your stock value, how foreign currency payments are recorded, and how credit for tax paid on import is matched. Product rules are outside this page, so check them for what you import.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Small retailers on credit</h2>
      <p>Many buyers may be small shops that pay in part, in cash or by UPI, often through a salesperson. Issue a receipt each time and enter it against the right party. Otherwise balances become arguments that nobody can settle.</p>
      <p class="para">See {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-traders/", "accounting for traders" %} for party ledgers and credit periods.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="area-title">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2 id="area-title" class="first-h2">About the market, and about us</h2>
    <p>Wikipedia describes Sadar Bazaar as primarily a wholesale market made up of smaller markets, including Pratap Market, Swadeshi Market and Teliwara. It describes Swadeshi Market as a cluster of small shops selling toys, artificial jewellery, gifts and plastic goods. Markets change, so treat this as a description and not a survey.</p>
    <p>AccountOrbit has no office in Sadar Bazaar, and takes enquiries by phone from anywhere in Delhi. This page explains the trade. See {% routeLink "/guides/how-to-hire-an-accountant-in-delhi/", "how to hire an accountant in Delhi" %}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

